  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a2/X-Men_Vol_1_5.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The X-Men arrive back home with heavy hearts, seeing as how their leader, Professor X, has lost his mind power (at the conclusion of the last issue).&nbsp; But they don't have much time to think about it, especially with the sudden arrival of Jean's parents, who've come to see the Professor and get a tour of the school.&nbsp; But Scott is accidentally closed in the Danger Room, which immediately begins to attack him (the purpose of which seems to be no more than to consume a page of story).&nbsp; He exits in time to see Mr. and Mrs. Grey leaving.\n\nBut as they drive away, we see Mastermind out, trying to find the X-Men, to no avail.&nbsp; Magneto summons him back to \"Asteroid M\" - a \"jagged asteroid, circling the Earth\" - but it has a secret base on it where Magneto has his headquarters.&nbsp; They've been searching for the X-Men for \"weeks\" but have found no trace.&nbsp; (Umm...<i>what</i>?&nbsp; See note about timing in grade details below...)&nbsp; Since the X-Men are \"sworn to protect mankind,\" they need to take them out of the picture, and he reminds all of them, particularly Quicksilver and the Scarlet Witch that the humans \"would <i>destroy</i> you if they could!\"&nbsp; He has a plan, which requires the Toad's involvement.\n\nSoon, the X-Men are watching a track meet on TV and note that one of the participants is leaping easily over all the other contestants.&nbsp; He <i>has</i> to be a mutant!&nbsp; Even the crowd watching the event can tell something fishy is going on and call him a \"fake!\"&nbsp; The X-Men realize that he'll be mobbed by the crowd if they don't do something to intervene!&nbsp; As they leave, the Professor reminds them that he won't be able to intervene without his powers.\n\nThe X-Men arrive and hold off the mob long enough to escape into a subway where Hank finally has a chance to get a good look at the athlete and concludes that he <i>has</i> to be the Toad, who then reveals himself.&nbsp; They realize that they almost took him back to their secret headquarters, which would have revealed their location to Magneto!&nbsp; He arrives on the scene at that moment with the rest of the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants, and they begin battling.&nbsp; Finally, Magneto snags the Angel by wrapping him in iron and orders them to withdraw.\n\nBack on the orbiting asteroid, Magneto tries to get Angel to reveal the location of the X-Men's headquarters, even shocking the Scarlet Witch by suggesting that \"only death will free you from me!\"&nbsp; She can't believe he would actually <i>murder</i> Angel.&nbsp; Back on Earth, the Toad seems like he's in a trance, like he can't believe Magneto would desert him, and he summons one of Magneto's \"drone pick-up capsules\" to take him back up to the asteroid, which all the other X-Men board as well.\n\nThe Brotherhood seem surprised when he returns, and the X-Men know that it's thus not a trap.&nbsp; But Magneto is able to control all the metal on the ship and prepares to eject them into space, only to see his control board short-circuited by the Scarlet Witch, who won't stand idly by!&nbsp; But even as Magneto turns to deal with the twins, the delay has allowed the X-Men to arrive.&nbsp; Cyclops detects the indecision by the pair and tries to reach out to them, but the hot-headed Quicksilver has no more love for the X-Men than he does Magneto.&nbsp; \"We can't trust <i>anyone</i>!\"\n\nBut at that moment, Magneto senses that the asteroid has gone off course and is starting to break up, and the last couple of pages are devoted to them all getting off of it without being destroyed.&nbsp; The X-Men manage to return to Earth in one of the drones but discover when they arrive that the drone is returning <i>somewhere</i> - Magneto must still be up there controlling it.&nbsp; \"Magneto can control its course <i>mentally</i>!&nbsp; There's no telling <i>where</i> he'll land!\"&nbsp; They realize they'll eventually be up against the evil mutants again someday.&nbsp; But for today, they've proved their mettle.\n\nWhen they return to the school, they find the Professor has been monitoring them mentally the whole time.&nbsp; It turns out that he never actually <i>lost</i> his powers.&nbsp; He reminds them that \"this is a <i>school</i>!&nbsp; And you can't graduate from any school without passing your final exam!\"&nbsp; They've just <i>taken</i> that exam - just as he planned it, and they've all passed.&nbsp; \"You've proven you can think and act for yourselves!&nbsp; Your training period is over!&nbsp; <i>Congratulations, my X-Men</i>!!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "not quite a mid-grade read.&nbsp; The Brotherhood of Evil Mutants is interesting enough, and the introduction seems to have gone over so well with the readers that Stan and Jack decided to bring them back in the very next issue (at least according to the splash page - this is a bi-monthly book after all, so it's plausible).&nbsp; The band of characters Magneto has pulled together <i>clearly</i> has no love between them and all simply serve Magneto out of fear (Toad), obligation (Quicksilver/Scarlet Witch) or convenience (Mastermind).&nbsp; And obviously Magneto has <i>zero</i> regard for their lives either, pointing out time and again that he's the strongest mutant - thus they <i>better</i> obey!&nbsp; We're expected to sympathize with the twins, and even Cyclops can sense how torn they are to be associated with Magneto.&nbsp; And I award positive points for the characterization of the group, if not for the rationale of them staying together.\n\nThis story gets major points off for the seeming disregard that the Professor had for their lives in his \"final exam.\"&nbsp; It's a case of \"you either pass the exam or you <i>die</i>!\"&nbsp; This is a school, so one would assume that a momentary failure would be turned into a \"teaching moment,\" and not death.&nbsp; So I'm sorry, but that did <i>not</i> work for me at all!&nbsp; And lest someone think that perhaps he could have intervened to <i>prevent</i> a death, what on Earth could he have done from the ground while his X-Men were up in space on the asteroid?&nbsp; The whole thing comes across as very callous.&nbsp; The Professor is all smiles at the end of the issue, but boy, what were his X-Men <i>thinking</i> after that, after he gambled their lives for the sake of a test?\n\nAnd speaking of the asteroid, it <i>does</i> seem an awfully fancy hideout for an evil bad guy, but Magneto might actually be the only one powerful enough to have legitimately pulled this off, so I'm okay with it and don't take any points off for the James Bond-like villain headquarters.&nbsp; (Hey, it's better even than the castle we've seen Dr. Doom hanging out in!&nbsp; And at this early stage in Marvel's development, can you identify any other baddies out there that even <i>approach</i> their threat level?&nbsp; I don't consider Sub-Mariner as truly bad, so he's disqualified!&nbsp; No, these two are <i>clearly</i> the cream of the crop when it comes to villains.&nbsp; This is even alluded to in the [LINK:1964:FF_27|FF #27|2027-07-04] letters/announcements page.)\n\nThe timing on the issue is once again muddled.&nbsp; On the splash page, it's made clear that the X-Men are just arriving home after their last adventure, with the now-powerless Professor X, yet on page 8, Magneto says they haven't found any traces of the X-Men even \"after weeks of searching.\"&nbsp; <i>What?</i>  So the story is telling us that it took \"weeks\" for the X-Men to return home?&nbsp; This is obviously a blunder, and the second one of similar type in a mere 5 issues (see the timeline problem I pointed out in issue #2 as well).\n\nOn page 15, Cyclops is holding the Toad with both arms, yet is still able to shoot his eye beams at Quicksilver, who thinks \"I didn't realize he could use his power beam while I was holding his arms!\"&nbsp; Interesting, because I'm almost certain that at this early stage, Cyclops <i>did</i> have to manipulate his visor with a hand.&nbsp; I'll have to pay attention going forward...\n\nI note that once again, the Scarlet Witch is anything but scarlet on the cover, but perhaps it's because both here and in issue #4, she's standing either behind or just beside Magneto, whose costume is red.&nbsp; Perhaps the colorist thought that they needed to be different colors to avoid blending together, even though on the inside, her costume leans more toward pink than Magneto's solid red one.&nbsp; Still, it's a bit weird to see a \"Scarlet\" Witch dressed in green on both covers.&nbsp; (Heh, and it seems I'm not the only one to have noticed this, as evidenced by Rocky Midyett's letter in XMEN #7 where he says \"By the way, if the Scarlet Witch is scarlet, how come she's always green on the cover?\")\n\nAlso, Hank's glove situation has not changed.&nbsp; He's sometimes drawn with them; sometimes without...back and forth throughout the issue...&nbsp; This might simply be the way it's going to appear in all the books - again, I'll keep an eye out, but it might happen so frequently that I might end up not pointing it out anymore.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME</b>",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}